        "text": "A bill can reach the floor through either suspension of the rules or regular order.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Suspension of the rules requires a two-thirds majority in Congress to pass.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Regular order involves a rule that permits amendments to the bill.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Amendments allow modifications to a bill.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker advocated for a modified version of the bill.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Conservatives should prefer the ability to amend and modify legislation.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Some individuals believed they could have blocked the bill early by preventing it from reaching the floor under a singular rule.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "If Republicans had joined Democrats to oppose the rule, the Chuck Schumer bill would have been discharged on the floor.",
